This chapter, including the districting map, may be amended from time to time by the city council, but no amendment shall become effective unless it shall have been proposed by or shall have first been submitted to the planning commission for review and recommendation. The planning commission shall have 30 days within which to submit its report. If the planning commission fails to submit a report within the 30-day period, it shall be deemed to have approved the proposed amendment.
(1) Initiation of amendments. Applications to amend this chapter may be in the form of proposals to amend the text or proposals to amend the districting map. An application may be initiated by the planning commission or be submitted to the planning commission by the city council or by any person who owns property within the districting jurisdiction of the city. Unless initiated by the city council or the planning commission, all applications for map amendments must be submitted by the owner of such property or the authorized agent of the owner for whom the redistricting is requested.
(2) Applications for amendments. Each request for amendment of the districting map shall be submitted on a form provided by the city. A separate request must be filed for each parcel or separate ownership for which a districting amendment is requested.
(3) Notice requirements. Before any changes or amendments to this chapter or the districting map, the following must be adhered to:
a. Notice by publication.
1. Prior to hearing. Notice by publication must occur at least 15 days, but not more than 45 days prior to the selected date of the hearing.
2. Pertains to. The notice by publication pertains to:
i. Adoption of a development standards ordinance;
ii. Amendment of a development standards ordinance;
iii. Amendment which enacts a redistricting of property from one classification to another (districting map amendment).
3. Notice contents. The notice must contain the following information regarding the hearing:
i. Time;
ii. Place;
iii. Purpose.
4. Notice contents (instigated by a party other than a local government). The notice of a hearing instigated by a party other than a local government must contain the following information:
i. Time;
ii. Place;
iii. Purpose;
iv. Location of property;
v. Current districting classification;
vi. Proposed districting classification.
b. Notice by posting a sign. If notice is given by posting a sign, the sign should contain the following information regarding the hearing:
1. Time;
2. Place;
3. Purpose;
4. Location of property
5. Current districting classification;
6. Proposed districting classification.
The sign shall be placed on the property to be considered for redistricting not less than 15 days prior to the hearing nor more than 45 days before the hearing.
(4) Procedures for the districting hearing. The following are procedures for the districting hearing:
a. Policies and procedures shall be set up and be committed to writing and available for distribution to the public;
b. A specific time shall be set up for the public hearing;
c. An agenda of the items to be heard shall be established and published;
d. There shall be limited time of equal duration for the proponents of the development standards legislation and the opponents of the development standards legislation.
In addition, minutes of the meeting should be maintained and the decision of the city be entered upon the minutes by official approval.
(Ord. of 3-9-1992, § 13.6)
